> On Mar 7, 2018, at 16:35, Ben Campbell <ben@nostrum.com> wrote:
>=20
>=20
>=20
>> On Mar 7, 2018, at 2:49 PM, Sean Turner <sean@sn3rd.com> wrote:
>>=20
>>=20
>>=20
>>> On Mar 6, 2018, at 12:27, Ben Campbell <ben@nostrum.com> wrote:
>>>=20
>>> Ben Campbell has entered the following ballot position for
>>> draft-ietf-tls-tls13-26: Yes
>>>=20
>>> When responding, please keep the subject line intact and reply to =
all
>>> email addresses included in the To and CC lines. (Feel free to cut =
this
>>> introductory paragraph, however.)
>>>=20
>>>=20
>>> Please refer to =
https://www.ietf.org/iesg/statement/discuss-criteria.html
>>> for more information about IESG DISCUSS and COMMENT positions.
>>>=20
>>>=20
>>> The document, along with other ballot positions, can be found here:
>>> https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-tls13/
>>>=20
>>>=20
>>>=20
>>> =
----------------------------------------------------------------------
>>> COMMENT:
>>> =
----------------------------------------------------------------------
>>>=20
>>> There has clearly been a lot of work put into this. It's a =
surprisingly
>>> understandable document, given its length and the complexity of the =
subject. I
>>> am balloting yes, but I have a few minor comments and nits. None of =
these are
>>> showstoppers, so please do with them as you will.
>>>=20
>>> *** Substantive Comments:
>>>=20
>>> =C2=A74.1.2, first paragraph: " When a client first connects to a =
server, it is
>>> REQUIRED to send the
>>> ClientHello as its first message. "
>>>=20
>>> Is that intended to prohibit the use of STARTTLS or similar =
application layer
>>> patterns? (To be clear, this is not an objection, just a =
clarification request.)
>>=20
>> No - this is just how it works TLS - clients send the ClientHello =
message first ;)
>=20
> I assume your response to mean that the point is merely that =
ClientHello is the first message of the TLS handshake. As worded, I =
think some readers might interpret this to mean that the client can send =
no other data at any layer prior to ClientHello.

Well this is about TLS :) But, maybe r/its first message/its first TLS =
handshake message ?

>>> =C2=A74.1.2, legacy_compression_methods: "Note that TLS 1.3 servers =
might receive
>>> TLS 1.2 or prior
>>>    ClientHellos which contain other compression methods and MUST
>>>    follow the procedures for the appropriate prior version of TLS."
>>>=20
>>> Is that intended to require TLS 1.3 servers to always be willing and =
able to
>>> negotiate 1.2? =C2=A74.2.1 has a similar assertion:
>>>=20
>>> "If this extension is not present, servers which are compliant with
>>> this specification MUST negotiate TLS 1.2 or prior as specified in
>>> [RFC5246], even if ClientHello.legacy_version is 0x0304 or later."
>>>=20
>>> But =C2=A74.2.3 says:
>>>=20
>>> "Note that TLS 1.2 defines this extension differently.  TLS 1.3
>>> implementations willing to negotiate TLS 1.2 MUST behave in
>>> accordance with the requirements of [RFC5246] when negotiating that
>>> version."
>>>=20
>>> ... which seems inconsistent (noting that this paragraph talks about
>>> "implementations" rather than "servers", so perhaps there's a subtle =
difference?
>>=20
>> In short kinda, sorta yes: =C2=A7s4.2.1 includes the following:
>>=20
>>  Implementations of TLS 1.3 which choose to support prior versions of
>>  TLS SHOULD support TLS 1.2.
>=20
> That still says =E2=80=9Cwhich choose to support=E2=80=9D

Right so there might be some implementations that choose to not support =
prior versions.

>> Not sure it=E2=80=99s inconsistent given that the 2nd quote is about =
the server needs to do with the information it=E2=80=99s getting from =
the client.
>=20
> I=E2=80=99m still confused. Is the server allowed to refuse to =
negotiate 1.2? Or is the exception in =C2=A74.2.1 supposed to apply to =
all of the related MUSTs?

Yes - TLS servers are allowed to refuse to negotiate earlier version; =
that=E2=80=99s deciding to not follow the SHOULD in  =C2=A7s4.2.1.  If =
they negotiate an earlier version then do that version that=E2=80=99s =
what=E2=80=99s in =C2=A7s4.1.2 and in =C2=A74.2.3.

>>> =C2=A74.2.1.1: The section is marked for removal. Do you expect that =
RFC
>>> implementations will ever need to interop with draft =
implementations? If so,
>>> the information in this section may continue to be useful for some =
time.
>>=20
>> I think it=E2=80=99ll be useful for about as long as it takes for =
them to rev their code bases, which I am sure hoping is faster than the =
6 or so weeks it=E2=80=99ll take for this draft to get to through the =
RFC editor=E2=80=99s queue.
>=20
> Okay.
>=20
>>=20
>>> =C2=A7D.5: This section has a lot of normative requirements that =
seem important. I
>>> wonder why it has been relegated to an appendix.
>>=20
>> =C2=A7D.5 is about backward compatibility and though we negotiations =
with 1.2 is a SHOULD we say nada about earlier versions.  And, we =
don=E2=80=99t want to say anything about earlier versions.   And, some =
of this is technically repeated from other RFCs, eg. 5768 and 6176 =
saying don=E2=80=99t do SSL2/3. So, it ended up in an appendix.
>=20
> Okay.
>=20
>>=20
>>> *** Editorial Comments and nits:
>>>=20
>>> =C2=A72: "If (EC)DHE key establishment
>>> is in use, then the ServerHello contains a "key_share" extension =
with
>>> the server=E2=80=99s ephemeral Diffie-Hellman share which MUST be in =
the same
>>> group as one of the client=E2=80=99s shares. "
>>>=20
>>> missing comma prior to "which=E2=80=9D.
>>=20
>> (grammar police are banging on my door as we speak)
>=20
> Well, my comment was labeled as editorial or nit :-)
>=20
>> So is the which clause restrictive or non restrictive?  I=E2=80=99m =
going with this this clause being restrictive (hence no comma).
>=20
> I=E2=80=99ll let the grammar police at your door debate the merits of =
=E2=80=9Cwhich=E2=80=9D vs =E2=80=9Cthat=E2=80=9D for restrictive =
clauses :-)

See the response to Adam=E2=80=99s comment :). But, yeah the RFC editor =
will definitely school me when the time comes.

>>> =C2=A74.1.1: "Note that if the PSK can be used without (EC)DHE then =
non-
>>> overlap in the "supported_groups" parameters need not be fatal, as =
it
>>> is in the non-PSK case discussed in the previous paragraph."
>>>=20
>>> I read "need not be fatal" to mean that it may still be fatal at =
times. Is that
>>> the intent?
>>=20
>> Yes that is the intent.
>=20
> Okay. Would it be reasonable to offer guidance about when might want =
to treat this as fatal even though it would have been possible to use =
the PSK without (EC)DHE?

So the fatal error is when the client and server are trying to negotiate =
(EC)DHE parameters and there=E2=80=99s no overlap in the =
supported_groups.  This would occur in the =E2=80=9Cnormal=E2=80=9D case =
when the client is anonymous (i.e., client only sends =
ClientHello+key_share and no psk_key_exchange_modes/pre_shared_key) or =
when the client is using PSK+(EC)DHE for client authentication.  But, =
that is described in the previous paragraph.  Maybe another way to say =
it is: normally you=E2=80=99d throw an error if supported_groups =
didn=E2=80=99t overlap, but here since you=E2=80=99re not using them =
because you=E2=80=99re doing PSK-only you don=E2=80=99t.

>>> =C2=A711: The IANA considerations have a number of constructions =
similar to "SHALL
>>> update/has updated". Is that text expected to collapse to "has =
updated" at some
>>> point?
>>=20
>> Yes - once we=E2=80=99ve gotten the a-okay from IANA, well as the RFC =
editor to make it just say =E2=80=9Chas updated=E2=80=9D etc.
>=20
> Okay.
>=20
>>=20
